\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}

The cosine compression--crossing-rate association is correlational; causal confirmation (e.g., whitening shallow representations) is future work. Information-theoretic alternatives such as MDL probing~\citep{voita2020information} evaluate representations via description length rather than threshold-based classification, potentially circumventing the crossing-rate bias mechanism identified here. Whether MDL-based layer selection converges with our threshold-dependent consensus remains an empirical question for future work. Whether quadratic residualization removes a confound or genuine position-correlated safety signal cannot be determined from the current design; disentangling these requires causal interventions on position encoding. Multi-layer aggregation (\cref{sec:related-work}) offers an architectural bypass of metric-dependent layer selection; our three-layer ensemble (\cref{tab:ensemble}) is a lightweight alternative. \textbf{Practical mitigation.} Three-level defense against metric-induced layer selection bias (\cref{app:practical_mitigation}): dual metric-family reporting, multi-layer ensemble ($p \leq 0.002$), or full cross-layer aggregation.

Layer selection metric choice can reverse a safety study's conclusion ($p{=}0.83$ vs.\ $p{<}0.005$, QwQ-32B); AdvBench replication confirms a geometric origin. The HS advantage is validated at 7--8B (+20--22pp, nested CV) and exploratory$^\dagger$ at 32B; single-metric probe conclusions should be treated as provisional.\looseness=-1

